From 0cbc6238d1da65a93e530de0766bce9d13d27726 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Angelo Arifi <arifilabs@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 21:52:18 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] tests: initialise non-contiguous tensors row by row
 (turboquant row f58ee0e97)

init_tensor_uniform wrote quantized data with a single packed
ggml_backend_tensor_set. That copies size bytes contiguously from tensor->data
and never strides by nb[1], so for a view whose rows are spaced over a wider
base, row i lands at i*row_size instead of i*nb[1] and the tail of the logical
extent keeps whatever the buffer held before. The reference then reads those
rows and produces NaN.

This was a LIVE defect in our tree, not a deferred capability: our
tests/test-backend-ops.cpp carried the packed write verbatim.

This is the ONLY one of lane-154's 25 not-attempted CAPABILITY rows that turned
out to be a real unclosed gap on the rig path. The other 24 are ruled in the
disposition table with per-row evidence; 18 of them have ZERO lines that are
both missing from us and still alive at turboquant's own tip.

Taken-from: llama-cpp-turboquant@f58ee0e97
Origin: applied verbatim; our init_tensor_uniform is otherwise identical here
Measured-effect: UNMEASURED as a delta - the sweep baseline 26213/26260 is
  re-run after this lane's build and compared case-for-case. The source notes
  the fix does NOT make their TQ4_1S k_v=1600 case pass; it unmasks a separate
  CUDA defect, which is off-rig for us.
---
 tests/test-backend-ops.cpp | 19 ++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 18 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/tests/test-backend-ops.cpp b/tests/test-backend-ops.cpp
index c880ed118..13ef52e0e 100644
--- a/tests/test-backend-ops.cpp
+++ b/tests/test-backend-ops.cpp
@@ -129,7 +129,24 @@ static void init_tensor_uniform(ggml_tensor * tensor, float min = -1.0f, float m
                 }
             }
         }
-        ggml_backend_tensor_set(tensor, dataq.data(), 0, dataq.size());
+        // A non-contiguous tensor (e.g. a k_v view whose rows are strided over a
+        // wider base) must be written row by row. ggml_backend_tensor_set copies
+        // `size` bytes contiguously from tensor->data, so a single packed write
+        // lays row i at i*row_size instead of i*nb[1], leaving the tail of the
+        // logical extent holding whatever was there before.
+        if (!ggml_is_contiguous(tensor) && ggml_n_dims(tensor) >= 2) {
+            const size_t row_sz = ggml_row_size(tensor->type, tensor->ne[0]);
+            const int64_t nrows = ggml_nrows(tensor);
+            for (int64_t r = 0; r < nrows; r++) {
+                const int64_t i3 =  r / (tensor->ne[1]*tensor->ne[2]);
+                const int64_t i2 = (r / tensor->ne[1]) % tensor->ne[2];
+                const int64_t i1 =  r % tensor->ne[1];
+                const size_t off = i1*tensor->nb[1] + i2*tensor->nb[2] + i3*tensor->nb[3];
+                ggml_backend_tensor_set(tensor, dataq.data() + r*row_sz, off, row_sz);
+            }
+        } else {
+            ggml_backend_tensor_set(tensor, dataq.data(), 0, dataq.size());
+        }
     } else if (tensor->type == GGML_TYPE_I8 || tensor->type == GGML_TYPE_I16) {
         // This is going to create some weird integers though.
         ggml_backend_tensor_set(tensor, data.data(), 0, nels * ggml_type_size(tensor->type));
